<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/expert/distributed-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A distributed system is many machines cooperating as one — and it must work
    <strong>while parts of it are broken, slow, or lying</strong>. Peter Deutsch catalogued the
    traps ("the fallacies of distributed computing"): the network is <em>not</em> reliable, latency
    is <em>not</em> zero, and topology <em>does</em> change. Everything in this subject is a
    disciplined answer to those five sentences.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five ideas that define the field" widget="equationStep" config={distCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts you will meet in production</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Eventual consistency:</strong> accept brief disagreement in exchange for always being up — the design behind DNS, shopping carts and social feeds. Strong consistency where it matters (payments), eventual everywhere else.</li>
    <li><strong>Idempotency:</strong> design operations so repeating them is safe ("set status to shipped" vs "decrement stock by one"). The network <em>will</em> resend; only idempotent handlers shrug.</li>
    <li><strong>Load balancing and caching:</strong> spread requests across replicas; remember answers. Two of the oldest tricks still deliver most of the performance in large systems.</li>
    <li><strong>Observability:</strong> distributed failures are invisible from any single machine. Logs, metrics and distributed tracing (following one request through 20 services) are how engineers see at all.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brewer CAP theorem (2000, proved 2012); Ongaro & Ousterhout, Raft (2014); Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications.</p>
</TopicPage>
